develop our own  ideas and we are in fact doing that  already  and compare that to the moderna  vaccine  back in cape town pietro tablanche is  still hoping moderna comes on board as  this would speed up the process modena  has announced a patent waiver while the  pandemic is still ongoing but afterwards  no commercialization will be possible  without its approval  we would like to have a voluntary  license to be able to transfer this  technology to other low and middle  income countries to use the platform for  other vaccines hiv tb ebola flu this hub  and the capacity capability we're  building here  is is beyond covert so this this in fact  is part of a strategy from from africa  to produce 60 of our vaccines by 2040.  so this is part of building an industry  today most vaccines used on the  continent are imported afrijin and its  partners aim to bring the first  homegrown products to market within  three  years and now i'd like to bring in  candace sahoma she is an access campaign  officer for doctors without borders and  she joins us now from johannesburg a  very warm welcome to the show candace  how optimistic are you that an african  developed mrna vaccine would make covert  19 shots more available across the  continent  yeah thank you so much for having me i  think i am very optimistic and you know  it's something that we have been calling  for as a civil society um looking at the  current um disparities when it comes to  vaccine access in particular in africa  we have seven percent of our population  only vaccinated um having a broader  manufacturing of these  vaccines would enable access it would  you know enable um as as you might know  that you know an issue that we encounter  in in africa in particular is an issue  around supply and broadening  manufacturing would enable um africa to  be able to be more independent and  supply um it's it's it's it's countries  or its people um to get vaccinated now  they're still working on this project  but time is of 